GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 67(6) the decision of the Chief Justice is final` MEANS
    IF `it is the decision of the Chief Justice on an appeal under subsection (5)`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the sentence is about "The decision of the Chief Justice" under subsection (5)
    ELSE NOTHING

-- src:2179 | 68.—(1) A judicial officer shall not be liable to be sued for any act
-- src:2180 | done by the judicial officer in the discharge of his or her judicial duty
-- src:2181 | whether or not within the limits of his or her jurisdiction, provided
-- src:2182 | that the judicial officer at the time in good faith believed himself or
-- src:2183 | herself to have jurisdiction to do or order the act complained of.
@ref s 68(1) (src:2179-2183)
GIVEN `the act was done by the judicial officer in the discharge of his or her judicial duty` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the judicial officer at the time in good faith believed himself or herself to have jurisdiction to do or order the act complained of` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 68(1) the judicial officer is liable to be sued for the act` MEANS
    IF `the act was done by the judicial officer in the discharge of his or her judicial duty` AND `the judicial officer at the time in good faith believed himself or herself to have jurisdiction to do or order the act complained of`
    THEN -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "A judicial officer shall not be liable to be sued for any act done by the judicial officer in the discharge of his or her judicial duty whether or not within the limits of his or her jurisdiction, provided that the judicial officer at the time in good faith believed himself or herself to have jurisdiction"
         JUST FALSE
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the immunity is for an act "in the discharge of his or her judicial duty", "provided that" the officer believed in good faith that there was jurisdiction; where it fails the section does not say that the officer is liable
    ELSE NOTHING

-- src:2185 | (2) No officer of a State Court charged with the duty of executing
-- src:2186 | any writ, summons, warrant, order, notice or other mandatory process
-- src:2187 | of the State Courts shall be liable to be sued —
-- src:2189 | (a) for the execution of or attempting to execute such writ,
-- src:2190 | summons, warrant, order, notice or other mandatory
-- src:2191 | process; or
-- src:2193 | (b) in respect of any damage caused to any property in
-- src:2194 | effecting or attempting to effect execution,
-- src:2196 | unless the officer knowingly acted in excess of the authority
-- src:2197 | conferred upon him or her by such writ, summons, warrant, order,
-- src:2198 | notice or other mandatory process of the court in question.
@ref s 68(2)(a) and (b) (src:2185-2198)
GIVEN `the officer of a State Court is charged with the duty of executing the writ, summons, warrant, order, notice or other mandatory process` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the suit is for the execution of or attempting to execute the process, or for damage caused to property in effecting or attempting to effect execution` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the officer knowingly acted in excess of the authority conferred by the process` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 68(2) the officer is liable to be sued` MEANS
    BRANCH
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the immunity is of an "officer of a State Court charged with the duty of executing" process, in respect of the matters in paragraphs (a) and (b)
        IF NOT (`the officer of a State Court is charged with the duty of executing the writ, summons, warrant, order, notice or other mandatory process` AND `the suit is for the execution of or attempting to execute the process, or for damage caused to property in effecting or attempting to effect execution`) THEN NOTHING
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "unless the officer knowingly acted in excess of the authority conferred"; where the officer did, the bar is lifted and a lifted bar grants nothing (QA)
        IF `the officer knowingly acted in excess of the authority conferred by the process` THEN NOTHING
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "No officer of a State Court charged with the duty of executing any writ ... shall be liable to be sued — (a) for the execution of or attempting to execute such writ ...; or (b) in respect of any damage caused to any property in effecting or attempting to effect execution"
        OTHERWISE JUST FALSE

@ref s 68(2) (src:2185-2198)
GIVEN `the officer of a State Court is charged with the duty of executing the writ, summons, warrant, order, notice or other mandatory process` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the suit is for the execution of or attempting to execute the process, or for damage caused to property in effecting or attempting to effect execution` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the officer knowingly acted in excess of the authority conferred by the process` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 68(2) the bar on suing the officer applies` MEANS
    IF `the officer of a State Court is charged with the duty of executing the writ, summons, warrant, order, notice or other mandatory process` AND `the suit is for the execution of or attempting to execute the process, or for damage caused to property in effecting or attempting to effect execution` AND NOT (`the officer knowingly acted in excess of the authority conferred by the process`)
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): the immunity is "unless the officer knowingly acted in excess of the authority" (an "unless" lifts the bar), and is of an officer charged with executing process in respect of (a) and (b)
         JUST FALSE

-- src:2202 | (3) An officer of a State Court is not to be deemed to have acted
-- src:2203 | knowingly in excess of his or her authority merely by reason of the
-- src:2204 | existence of a dispute as to the ownership of any property seized
-- src:2205 | under any enforcement order or writ of distress.
@ref s 68(3) (src:2202-2205)
GIVEN `the only ground is the existence of a dispute as to the ownership of property seized under an enforcement order or writ of distress` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 68(3) the officer is deemed to have acted knowingly in excess of his or her authority` MEANS
    IF `the only ground is the existence of a dispute as to the ownership of property seized under an enforcement order or writ of distress`
    THEN -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "An officer of a State Court is not to be deemed to have acted knowingly in excess of his or her authority merely by reason of the existence of a dispute as to the ownership of any property seized under any enforcement order or writ of distress"
         JUST FALSE
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the sentence says only that the dispute alone does not amount to knowing excess ("merely by reason of")
    ELSE NOTHING

-- src:2211 | (4) No judicial officer, officer of a State Court or court‑appointed
-- src:2212 | mediator shall be liable to be sued for an act done by him or her for
-- src:2213 | the purposes of any mediation or other alternative dispute resolution
-- src:2214 | process conducted by him or her in a State Court, if the act —
-- src:2216 | (a) was done in good faith; and
-- src:2218 | (b) did not involve any fraud or wilful misconduct on his or her
-- src:2219 | part.
@ref s 68(4)(a) and (b) (src:2211-2219)
GIVEN `the person is a judicial officer, an officer of a State Court or a court-appointed mediator` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the act was done for the purposes of a mediation or other alternative dispute resolution process conducted by the person in a State Court` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the act was done in good faith` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the act did not involve any fraud or wilful misconduct on the person's part` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 68(4) the person is liable to be sued for the act` MEANS
    IF `the person is a judicial officer, an officer of a State Court or a court-appointed mediator`
       AND `the act was done for the purposes of a mediation or other alternative dispute resolution process conducted by the person in a State Court`
       AND `the act was done in good faith`
       AND `the act did not involve any fraud or wilful misconduct on the person's part`
    THEN -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "No judicial officer, officer of a State Court or court‑appointed mediator shall be liable to be sued for an act done by him or her for the purposes of any mediation or other alternative dispute resolution process conducted by him or her in a State Court, if the act — (a) was done in good faith; and (b) did not involve any fraud or wilful misconduct"
         JUST FALSE
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the immunity is "if the act" meets both (a) and (b); where it does not, the section does not say that the person is liable
    ELSE NOTHING

§ `Section 69 -- Rules of Court`

-- Section 69 is the power to make Rules of Court: machinery, inert (reason 2).
